# Point ideals of a Weierstrass curve determine the point

Theorem statement: `WeierstrassCurve.Affine.CoordinateRing.XYIdeal_eq_XYIdeal_iff`
(`0b9a22c4-27e0-5527-8114-51326c8a5ad7`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `167f3628-0cf7-58a1-9a98-1ac1c13d4c8a`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $F$ be a field and let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve over $F$ in affine coordinates, with
associated bivariate polynomial $W.\mathrm{polynomial} \in F[X][Y]$ and coordinate ring $F[W] =
F[X][Y]/(W.\mathrm{polynomial})$, the quotient map being `mk W`. For $x \in F$ and $y \in F[X]$,
`XYIdeal W x y` denotes the ideal of $F[W]$ generated by the classes of $C(X - C\,x)$ and of $Y -
C\,y$. Assume $x_1, y_1 \in F$ satisfy the Weierstrass equation of $W$, i.e. `W.Equation x₁ y₁`, and
let $x_2, y_2 \in F$ be arbitrary elements, with no assumption that $(x_2,y_2)$ lies on $W$. The
assertion is the equivalence: the ideals $\big(X - x_1,\, Y - y_1\big)$ and $\big(X - x_2,\, Y -
y_2\big)$ of $F[W]$ coincide if and only if $x_1 = x_2$ and $y_1 = y_2$. Note that the second
coordinates are taken to be the constant polynomials $C\,y_1$ and $C\,y_2$, so both ideals are the
ideals attached to $F$-rational points.

This is the injectivity half of the classical correspondence between $F$-rational affine points of a
plane curve and the maximal ideals of its coordinate ring that they cut out. It is used in the
construction of a centred place datum for a genus-one Weierstrass curve in
[`WeierstrassCurve.Affine.exists_genusOnePlaceGate_isCentred_abelTheorem`]
(thm.html#WeierstrassCurve.Affine.exists_genusOnePlaceGate_isCentred_abelTheorem).

